<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Coding agents can answer analytical questions over a data lake by discovering, cleaning, and joining the relevant data. However, they discard this work after each question and redo it from scratch for the next one. Tool evolution, which automatically builds and refines reusable tools across tasks, is a natural fit for this repetition. Since a ground-truth label for a data-lake question requires an expert to solve it first, we consider the unsupervised setting. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ool <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>volution for <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>nalytics over data <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>akes), which evolves a <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">library of tools and usage skills</span> for a given Worker agent without labels. Two agents propose tools from complementary evidence, an Explorer from the data lake itself and a Critic from Worker trajectories. A proposed change is kept only if the Worker adopts the tool on a rerun without extra steps and any changed answer is supported by trajectory evidence. We also introduce <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span>, which generates synthetic questions for tool evolution when real ones are unavailable. We evaluate on KramaBench and LakeQA with four Worker LLMs under step budgets, which cap the number of steps the Worker may take per question. With a tight budget of 10 or 15 steps, <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> improves every Worker on both benchmarks, lifting Sonnet-5 at 15 steps by 9.3 points on KramaBench and 21.7 points on LakeQA. A tighter budget also yields better feedback for evolution. For example, Sonnet-5’s LakeQA library evolved at 20 steps and deployed unchanged at 30 steps scores 68.3, above both the baseline (59.6) and a library evolved directly at 30 steps (64.2). Additionally, a library evolved on synthetic questions performs comparably to one evolved on real questions. These results show that useful tools can be evolved without labels, and hopefully inspire future work on what to evolve and how to obtain feedback in unsupervised agent evolution.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A data lake <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nargesian et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> stores a large, heterogeneous collection of tables, documents, and raw files. Answering analytical questions over a data lake requires discovering relevant data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernandez et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castelo et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, transforming messy records into structured representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kandel et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and integrating data across sources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Freire et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Coding agents equipped with a bash shell and a file editor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> can automate this workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayan et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernandez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, they typically treat each question over the same data lake in isolation and discard every parser, schema check, and join script as soon as the question ends.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an analyst asking which metropolitan area accounts for the largest proportion of its state’s fraud reports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. The answer lies in 52 per-state CSV files of metropolitan-area counts with irregular headers, hidden among the data lake’s thousands of files next to similar national and state summaries. Without a catalog or index, table granularity cannot be inferred from filenames, so a coding agent may pick a national summary table that lacks state breakdowns. Even when the agent locates the 52 state files, opening and cleaning them one by one exhausts a 30-step budget and fails sooner under a 15-step budget. When the next question comes, the agent repeats the same manual exploration and cleaning from scratch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Database systems avoid repeated scans of raw data by maintaining catalogs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halevy et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Graefe, 2024</a>)</cite>, and views <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halevy, 2001</a>)</cite>. Coding agents can follow the same principle with reusable <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tools</em> that replace repeated step-by-step exploration with a single call. Some of these tools are backed by precomputed <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data artifacts</em>, such as catalogs and indexes, and <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">usage skills</em> tell the agent when and how to invoke each tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, existing methods cannot automatically generate such tools, for two reasons.
First, catalog and ontology formats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> store descriptive metadata rather than executable tools for cleaning and querying data, whereas code tool generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite> build tools for the task at hand, not artifacts that pay off only across many questions.
Second, supervised tool generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and self-evolving ontologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chong et al., 2026</a>)</cite> rely on ground-truth validation labels, which are costly to obtain in data-lake analytics because verifying even one answer requires an expert to manually discover, clean, and join the underlying data sets first. Without ground-truth labels, keeping every tool that runs without a runtime error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite> is unreliable because a tool can execute cleanly while reading a table at the wrong granularity, dropping rows during parsing, or joining tables on unaligned keys.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> (<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ool <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>volution for <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>nalytics over data <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>akes), an unsupervised framework that evolves a <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">library</em> for a fixed Worker agent. The library consists of executable tools, some backed by precomputed data artifacts, and usage skills. At test time, the library is frozen and provided to the Worker to answer new questions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Contributions</h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool evolution from data exploration and Worker trajectories.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> builds the library offline from two complementary channels, which differ in the evidence they draw on. An <em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Explorer</em> agent inspects the data lake without seeing any question or trajectory and builds tools that a domain expert would prepare for the data lake, for example search and lookup tools backed by precomputed catalogs or cleaned tables. A <em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Critic</em> agent examines the Worker’s trajectories on training questions to find where the Worker wastes steps or makes errors, and creates or revises tools accordingly. A <em id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Skill Writer</em> agent then updates the usage skills to reflect every implemented change.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool verification without ground-truth answers.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because a newly proposed tool can still contain logic bugs or be ignored by the Worker, we introduce a rerun verification stage that accepts or rejects each change without answer labels. <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> reruns the Worker with the updated library on the questions that motivated the change. The change is kept only if the Worker uses the changed tool, still finishes without extra steps, and, whenever its answer changes, the trajectory evidence supports the new answer over the old one.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic question generation for tool evolution.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When real user questions are scarce for a data lake, we introduce <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> to generate questions that can be used by <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>, using a small set of seed questions as demonstrations. <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> first drafts a question from a cluster of related files, then hardens it over several revisions by adding data processing and reasoning demands, such as multi-table joins, while ensuring it is answerable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical evaluation across models and step budgets.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> on KramaBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and LakeQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> with four Worker backbones (Gemini-3.8-Flash, Gemini-3.1-Pro, Sonnet-5, and Opus-4.8) under varying step budgets, i.e., the maximum Worker steps per question. We make three main observations.
First, under tight 10- and 15-step budgets, <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> improves accuracy for every Worker on both benchmarks. With Sonnet-5 at 15 steps, it gains 9.3 points on KramaBench and 21.7 points on LakeQA, and also outperforms the execution-only tool evolution method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Second, libraries evolved under a tighter budget transfer to looser budgets. Sonnet-5’s LakeQA library evolved at 20 steps scores 68.3 at 30 steps, above both the baseline (59.6) and a library evolved at 30 steps (64.2), suggesting that tighter budgets give better feedback for evolution.
Third, evolving on 50 synthetic questions from <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> performs comparably to evolving on real questions, and our unsupervised rerun-based verification matches oracle ground-truth verification. The library evolved in the OpenHands harness also transfers unchanged to Google ADK, Codex CLI, and Prime Agent, improving accuracy by up to 16.3 points.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tools and Agents for Data Analytics.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data analytics over heterogeneous sources involves discovering relevant files <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paton et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, preparing and integrating their contents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and performing analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nam et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Prior work supports this with resources that humans prepare in advance, including data artifacts such as metadata catalogs and relationship indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halevy et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernandez et al., 2018</a>)</cite> or materialized views and indexes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2000</a>)</cite>, and tools such as user-defined plugins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or a fixed toolset for searching and inspecting the lake <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Recent work lets agents build such resources themselves. In EvoOntology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chong et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, a builder agent constructs and refines an ontology layer that data agents query through tools, but a refinement is kept only if the score on a held-out labeled split improves. Closest to our setting, DataCOPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> discovers skills without labels by scoring sampled trajectories through answer agreement, but its skills are procedural guidance, not callable tools backed by persistent artifacts. We study fully automatic tool evolution, where no human writes tools, curates artifacts, or labels answers, and agents decide from data exploration and unlabeled trajectories which tools to build and how to guide their use with skills.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool Evolution for LLM Agents.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Automatic tool creation for LLMs began with math and science questions that a single program can answer. A model writes a helper function, verified against labeled examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or by execution tests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and reuses it across questions of the same type. Agent systems extended this to tools built during long-horizon tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and usually stored for later use. These agents create a tool when a task cannot proceed without one, such as a transcript downloader or a web page fetcher, so the tool is exercised immediately, and acceptance reduces to whether it executes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, whether the environment reflects completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, or whether the agent judges its output plausible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. In this work, we study unsupervised tool evolution for data-lake analytics, where neither assumption holds. Here tools rarely fill a gap, since the Worker is not blocked without them, and they cannot be credited for a correct answer without labels. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> therefore verifies tools by the Worker’s behavior on a rerun, and we study how to make unsupervised tool evolution more effective, in particular by evolving under tighter step budgets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coding agents and tools.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider coding agents equipped with a shell and a file editor, which enable them to read and modify files and execute code. Beyond these basic capabilities, we define a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tool</em> as a named, callable program added to the agent’s action space and retained across tasks. Once implemented and debugged, a tool can be invoked in a single action without reconstructing its procedure. A tool may also rely on persistent artifacts, such as indexes or cached metadata, that are built once and reused across tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool evolution for data-lake analytics.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a natural-language question over a data lake, a coding agent, which we call the <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Worker</em>, autonomously discovers the relevant files and performs the data preparation, integration, analysis, and reasoning needed to answer the question under a step budget. Without access to ground-truth answers, a tool evolution system observes the Worker’s trajectories on training questions and produces a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">library</em> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=(\mathcal{T},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=(\mathcal{T},S)</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of a set of tools <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a skill file <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a natural-language document that tells the Worker when and how to use the tools. We then freeze the library and measure the Worker’s accuracy with it on a disjoint set of test questions over the same data lake.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27327v1/toolevo_pipeline.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/236;" width="476" height="236"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The <span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> evolution loop. The Worker first solves the questions with the current library (Stage 0). The Explorer proposes tools for the data lake as a whole (Stage 1), and the Critic proposes targeted fixes from the Worker’s trajectories (Stage 2). The Skill Writer then updates the usage guidance (Stage 3), and a rerun of the Worker decides which changes to keep (Stage 4). Robot icons mark LLM roles. No stage uses ground-truth answers.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Unsupervised Tool Evolution via <span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alongside the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Worker</em> that answers questions over the data lake, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> coordinates five LLM agents to evolve <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Explorer</em> inspects the data lake to propose data-specific tools and precomputed data artifacts. The <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Critic</em> diagnoses execution bottlenecks and errors in the Worker trajectories to propose targeted tool changes. The <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Toolsmith</em> implements proposals from the Explorer and Critic as executable code and validates them with unit tests. The <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Skill Writer</em> maintains <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that guides when and how to invoke each tool in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Analyst</em> compares original and rerun trajectories during verification whenever their final answers differ.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>The tool evolution pipeline</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During offline tool evolution, which we refer to as training even though no model weights are updated, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> processes the training questions in batches and runs the five stages in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Tool evolution for data-lake analytics. ‣ 3.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on each batch to iteratively update <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 0: Trajectory Collection</span>, the Worker executes a batch of questions with the current library <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each trajectory is recorded together with a summary of its tool usage as evidence for later stages. The summary covers which tools were used, which were unused, and where the Worker took many consecutive steps without any tool call. For example, on a question over a large directory or an Excel workbook, the summary flags that the Worker spent seven consecutive bash steps running file searches without calling any library tool and exhausted its step budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Expert Exploration</span>, an Explorer agent inspects the data lake and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> without access to task questions or Worker trajectories. It <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">acts as a domain expert</em> preparing the tools for the data lake as a whole rather than for any specific task, and proposes <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add tool</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">update tool</span> operations. It can also create tools backed by persistent artifacts that precompute information for reuse across tasks. A Toolsmith agent implements these proposals and checks them with executable unit tests. Unlike the other stages, this stage runs only once every <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> batches. For example, after surveying thousands of raw CSV and Excel files, the Explorer proposes a dataset search tool backed by a precomputed SQLite catalog of file formats, column headers, and sample rows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: Trajectory Criticism</span>, a Critic agent reviews the evidence collected in Stage 0 and the current tool set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, including those just added or updated by the Explorer, to propose <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">update tool</span> operations for defects in used tools, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">promote tool</span> operations for unused tools that could have helped, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add tool</span> operations for capability gaps that the current tools do not cover. The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add tool</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">update tool</span> proposals are implemented by the Toolsmith, and the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">promote tool</span> proposals are passed to the next skill update stage. For instance, when trajectories show the Worker failing on Excel sheets whose headers do not start on the first row, the Critic proposes <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">add tool</span> for a header-aware workbook loader, or proposes <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">update tool</span> when an existing catalog tool misses yearly editions of a table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3: Skill Update</span>, a Skill Writer agent updates the skill file <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by integrating the implemented changes from the Explorer and the Critic and the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">promote tool</span> proposals. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains two parts, the overall cross-tool guidance and the per-tool entries. For example, the Skill Writer adds a cross-tool workflow rule instructing the Worker to query the catalog tool before running shell searches, and documents when to invoke the workbook loader on multi-sheet Excel files.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 4: Verification</span>, the Worker reruns the questions with the updated library. Each rerun is compared with the original run on three checks, whether the changed tool was used, whether the task still finished, and whether any changed answer is better supported by the trajectory (Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Verification without ground-truth answers ‣ 3.2 Unsupervised Tool Evolution via TEAL ‣ 3 Method ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>). A change is kept if a rerun passes all three checks and reverted otherwise, after at most one round of repair. The resulting library <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> carries forward to the next batch and is frozen after offline training for evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Two complementary channels for tool evolution.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> combines two complementary channels, the Critic and the Explorer, because neither suffices alone. Prior tool-evolution methods rely only on trajectory analysis similar to our Critic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Trajectory analysis is effective for fixing specific execution errors, such as misread offset headers in Excel sheets or bugs in called tools. However, because the Critic only sees a small batch of trajectories at a time, it focuses on immediate fixes for those questions rather than building data lake-wide tools. The Explorer addresses this limitation. Without seeing any question or trajectory, it is instructed to act as a domain expert preparing tools for the whole data lake. It scans the lake’s directory structures, file formats, and schemas and proposes tools that serve many questions, such as search and lookup tools, some backed by precomputed data artifacts such as metadata catalogs and value indexes. These tools let the Worker locate relevant data in a single tool call. In short, the Critic asks <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what should change given how the Worker actually solved these tasks?</em>, while the Explorer asks <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what would a domain expert build for this data lake?</em></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Verification without ground-truth answers</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A proposed tool change can pass its unit tests and still fail to help the Worker. The tool may contain a logic error, such as reading a table at the wrong granularity, or the Worker may simply ignore it. <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Verification</em> is the stage that decides whether to keep or revert each change.
Because ground-truth answers are unavailable, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> cannot judge a change by whether it makes answers correct. Instead, it reruns the Worker with the updated library on the questions that motivated the change and compares each rerun with the original trajectory on three checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first check confirms that the Worker actually calls the changed tool during the rerun, since a tool that is never called cannot improve any answer. When the Worker ignores the tool, the reason is usually unclear guidance rather than a broken tool, so <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> asks the Skill Writer to revise the tool’s usage instructions and reruns the question before rejecting the change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second check confirms that the change does not make the Worker worse at finishing the question. A rerun fails this check if it produces no answer where the original run produced one, or if it uses more than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> steps beyond the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> steps of the original run.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The third check applies only when the rerun produces a different answer from the original run. In this case, an Analyst agent compares the two trajectories and judges which answer is better supported by the execution evidence, for example whether the final value appears in a tool output or is merely asserted by the Worker. The Analyst does not assume that the original answer is correct. The rerun fails this check only if the evidence favors the original answer, and passes if the evidence favors the new answer or is inconclusive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> keeps a change if at least one rerun passes all three checks and reverts it otherwise. In this way, verification goes beyond checking whether a tool runs without errors and uses the Worker’s own trajectories as evidence of whether the tool actually helped.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27327v1/qgen_pipeline.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/85;" width="476" height="85"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Synthetic question generation with <span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span>. Given the data lake and a small set of seed questions as demonstrations, <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> generates each question in four stages.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Synthetic Question Generation via <span id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Questions written by users are scarce and not always accessible. Existing data lake benchmarks contain only around a hundred questions each, and a newly deployed data lake may have none.
Because <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> does not need ground-truth answers, evolution needs only questions. We therefore propose <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span>, which generates synthetic questions from the data lake, using a small set of seed questions as demonstrations of style and difficulty.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.2 Verification without ground-truth answers ‣ 3.2 Unsupervised Tool Evolution via TEAL ‣ 3 Method ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> generates each question in four stages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">①Explore and Draft.</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> inspects a cluster of relevant data files and drafts a question with an answer, computed by running code on the data.
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">② Harden.</span> In up to three rounds, <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> makes the question harder, for example by adding a join across tables or a hard-to-parse file, and recomputes the answer after each round.
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">③ Build Pipeline.</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> turns the final solution into an executable pipeline and checks that it reproduces the computed answer. This confirms that the question is answerable from the data.
<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">④ Rewrite.</span> <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> rewrites the question in natural language, preserving essential definitions that are needed to make it unambiguous but omitting details that the Worker can discover itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> passes only the questions to <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>. The answers and solution pipelines serve only to keep each question well posed and answerable. As a result, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> does not need to verify that its answers are correct, which is a major challenge in synthetic data generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramrakhya et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gureja et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments address two research questions (RQs). <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1: Are agent-evolved tools useful?</span> We ask whether a library evolved without labels improves a coding agent’s accuracy on data-lake analytics, across Workers, datasets, and step budgets. <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2: How can feedback be used effectively without ground-truth answers?</span> We ask which signals, from step budget pressure to the proposal channels and rerun-based verification, make unsupervised evolution work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For KramaBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we merge the data lakes of all six domains (archaeology, astronomy, biomedical, environment, legal, and wildfire) into a single unified data lake of 1,742 files and use stratified sampling to select 51 training and 53 test tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For LakeQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we use its tables-only variant, which keeps the 130 questions whose final reasoning step relies on tables and folds any fact from removed non-table sources into the question text, preserving the original answers. We randomly split them into 50 training and 80 test tasks. Each requires one to five reasoning hops over a lake of 4,180 tables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We report LakeQA accuracy under its deterministic scorer and the official KramaBench score, which is exact match for most tasks and partial credit (F1 for lists, relative error for approximate numerics, an LLM judge for free-text answers) for the rest. We refer to both as accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27327v1/step_budget.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Accuracy versus Worker step budget in the real-question scenario. Rows correspond to benchmarks (top: KramaBench, bottom: LakeQA) and columns to Worker models. The horizontal axis is the step budget <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at test time, from 10 to 30 steps. The grey line is the baseline Worker without evolved tools. The blue line is <span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> with a library evolved and tested at the same budget <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The orange dashed line is a frozen library: it is evolved once at the ringed budget and then tested unchanged at each looser budget.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use OpenHands<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk</a>.</span></span></span> as the harness for all agents. To assess generalization across Worker backends, we evaluate Gemini-3.8-Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2026b</a>)</cite>, Gemini-3.1-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2026a</a>)</cite>, Sonnet-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026b</a>)</cite>, and Opus-4.8 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026a</a>)</cite>, while using Opus-4.8 for all evolution roles. Each Worker episode has a step budget of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: up to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-3</annotation></semantics></math> steps for the task, plus at most 3 steps to write an answer if none has been written by then. A step budget is the practical way to bound the cost and latency of answering a question, and how many steps a deployment can afford varies. We therefore treat it as an experimental variable. In all experiments, we use batches of 5 questions, run the Explorer on the first batch and every <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=4</annotation></semantics></math> batches after, and set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\max(2,\,0.2n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 0.2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\max(2,\,0.2n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the real training questions from the datasets, we sweep <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> over 10, 15, 20, 25, and 30 steps. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Datasets. ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> plots accuracy against <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the baseline without the evolved library and for <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>, reporting the mean and standard deviation over three test repeats. We have the following findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Under a tight step budget, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> improves accuracy substantially.</span> With Sonnet-5 as the Worker, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> improves accuracy by 22.6 points on KramaBench and 30.4 points on LakeQA at a budget of 10 steps, and by 9.3 and 21.7 points at 15 steps. The gain holds for all four Workers on both lakes at 10 and 15 steps. In five of the eight panels, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> at 15 steps is on par with or above the baseline at 30 steps. We attribute the improvement to the fact that a single tool call replaces a multi-step procedure, such as locating, loading, and joining the relevant files, so the Worker can finish the task within a budget that the baseline exhausts on file discovery. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Qualitative Analysis ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> illustrates this with two test tasks, and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Tool Statistics ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> lists the most used tools.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Under a loose budget, a library evolved at that budget does not always help, but one evolved under a tighter budget does.</span> At 20 to 30 steps, the library evolved at the deployment budget (blue line) sometimes falls below the baseline (grey line), whereas the library evolved under a tighter budget and deployed unchanged (orange line) stays above the baseline at every looser budget and is usually the best of the three. The difference lies in the feedback that evolution receives. Under a tight budget, the Worker fails in visible ways, through empty answers, truncated pipelines, and repeated processing work, which give the Critic concrete targets. A saved step then changes the rerun outcome, so verification can tell a useful tool from a useless one, and the short trajectories are also easier to analyze. Under a loose budget, the Worker solves most tasks inline, so a tool rarely changes the outcome, and the long trajectories make proposals harder to ground and reruns harder to compare, which leaves the quality of tools and skills unchecked. Step budget pressure thus acts as a training signal in place of gold labels, which makes evolving under a tighter budget and deploying under a looser one a practical recipe.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Steps and Cost ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> further reports the number of steps each Worker actually uses and the resulting cost per episode, which reflect the practical value of <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> in deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Comparison with Existing Methods</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also compare <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> with the agentic methods evaluated in KramaBench and LakeQA and with an existing tool evolution method. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DS-Guru</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2026</a>)</cite> writes a complete Python script from a data preview and repairs it with error feedback. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Smolagents DeepResearch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roucher et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al., 2026</a>)</cite> is a ReAct agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> that executes Python step by step with file inspection tools. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LakeQA Agent</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> is the reference agent released with LakeQA, which calls human-designed tools to search, inspect, and run Python, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LakeQA Agent + RAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> adds a retrieval tool backed by a pre-built index of the lake’s tables. <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ISE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>)</cite> evolves tools without gold labels and keeps every tool that runs without errors, and we reproduce it from the released code and replace only its Worker with that of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>.</p>
</div>
