</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each existing method, we use the hyperparameters from its original evaluation on the corresponding dataset, including a 20-step budget for Smolagents and a 25-step budget for LakeQA Agent, so its result is the same in both columns. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Comparison with Existing Methods ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> leads to two observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First, our baseline Worker is already a strong coding agent. On KramaBench, it outperforms DS-Guru and Smolagents by a wide margin even at 15 steps, fewer than the 20 steps Smolagents is allowed. On LakeQA, it surpasses LakeQA Agent at 30 steps, with or without RAG, even though LakeQA Agent comes with human-designed tools and a pre-built retrieval index. <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> improves on this strong baseline on both datasets and at both budgets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we compare <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> with ISE, another unsupervised tool evolution method whose released code allows a faithful reproduction. ISE retains a tool once it executes successfully. Under the same Worker and the same 15-step budget, ISE underperforms <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> on both datasets (by 5.7 points on KramaBench), indicating that a tool that executes successfully does not necessarily help with the task. This issue is more pronounced on LakeQA, where ISE falls 10.0 points below the baseline. For instance, ISE evolved <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">search_file_content</span>, which scans the lake line by line. The tool was retained after a single successful run on a simple search, yet 121 of its 126 calls timed out at test time. The steps consumed by these calls prevented the Worker from reaching an answer within the step budget. Under execution-only verification, such a tool therefore does more harm than good.</p>
</div>
</section>
