<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the synthetic-question scenario, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span> generates 50 questions from the lake of each dataset. Evolution sees only these questions, without answers, and the resulting library is evaluated on the same held-out test tasks as in the real-question scenario. In Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.4 Results in the synthetic-question scenario ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the library evolved on synthetic questions improves Sonnet-5 over the baseline on both datasets and performs comparably to the library evolved on real questions. The same holds for all four Workers (Table <a href="#A4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix D Synthetic-Question Scenario Across Workers ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Synthetic-Question Scenario Across Workers ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Although the generated questions are never verified by a human, they exercise enough of the lake’s preparation and discovery work for <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> to build a library that transfers to real questions, so tool evolution can begin before any user question has been asked.</p>
</div>
</section>
