<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span> Accuracy (%) of ablations and reference configurations with Sonnet-5 as the Worker at a 15-step budget, in the real-question scenario.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.5 Ablation and Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> isolates the contribution of each component with Sonnet-5 as the Worker at a budget of 15 steps. Removing the Explorer leaves only the Critic channel, which still improves over the baseline but falls slightly short of the full <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>. Evolution without the Explorer also no longer produces the artifact-backed tools shown in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Qualitative Analysis ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, consistent with the complementary roles of the two channels (Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" title="Two complementary channels for tool evolution. ‣ 3.2.1 The tool evolution pipeline ‣ 3.2 Unsupervised Tool Evolution via TEAL ‣ 3 Method ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>). Removing the evolved skills causes a larger drop, because the Worker no longer knows how to use the tools. A good tool alone therefore does not guarantee a gain, and the tools and their usage skills must be evolved together.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also report two oracle variants that give <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> ground-truth labels during evolution, which our unsupervised setting does not have. Given only the ground-truth answers, <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> performs comparably to its label-free version, so our verification checks already obtain the feedback that the answers would provide. Given the full ground-truth solutions as well, the system knows how to solve the tasks and creates more targeted tools, such as <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">merge_entity_tables</span>, which joins tables on both state and metropolitan area and matches area names that differ only in punctuation, whereas the label-free library only standardizes state names with <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">normalize_join_state_keys</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To test generalization across harnesses, we take the library evolved under OpenHands and deploy it unchanged under three other harnesses, Google ADK<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/google/adk-python" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/google/adk-python</a>.</span></span></span>, Prime Agent<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent</a>.</span></span></span>, and Codex CLI<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/openai/codex</a>.</span></span></span>, with Sonnet-5 as the Worker in each. The library improves the Worker over its baseline under every harness, so the evolved tools and skills generalize beyond the harness they were evolved in.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we proposed <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>, a framework that evolves tools and usage skills for a coding agent over a data lake without ground-truth labels, and <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL-Q</span>, which generates synthetic questions for evolution. <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> proposes tools from both the data lake and the Worker’s trajectories, and verifies each change by checking the Worker’s tool use and trajectory evidence on a rerun, which performs comparably to verification with ground-truth answers. Our results show that unsupervised tool evolution is most effective under a tight step budget, which reveals where the Worker needs tools. Libraries evolved in this way improve all four Workers under tight budgets and remain effective under looser budgets, and libraries evolved on synthetic questions perform comparably to those evolved on real ones.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and future work.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evolved library of tools and skills introduces additional overhead in two forms. First, it lengthens the prompt that the Worker reads at every step, which increases the input tokens. Second, the Worker tends to reason more at each step, plausibly about which tools to call (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Steps and Cost ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). Future work may reduce the input overhead by improving cache reuse of repeated prompt content, and the reasoning overhead by internalizing the skills into the Worker.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
