<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Qualitative Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tools with build artifacts.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As introduced in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the Explorer may create a tool backed by a persistent artifact that is constructed once, stored alongside the tool, and consulted at call time. We illustrate this mechanism with <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">search_dataset_catalog</span>, the discovery tool of the Sonnet-5 LakeQA library evolved under a budget of 10 steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Its artifact is a catalog of the lake, built in a single pass over every directory that infers the true format of each file from its bytes, records the header, an estimated row count, per-column samples, and data-quality flags, and embeds the name of each directory. The pass takes approximately four minutes of wall-clock time and roughly forty batched embedding calls, and the resulting 93 MB SQLite catalog is stored beside the tool and never rebuilt inside a task. The tool itself is a ranked search over this catalog. It takes a natural-language description of the data sought and returns the best-matching directories. The tool was revised fourteen times, each revision traceable to a Worker trajectory. For instance, after a Worker had to fall back to <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#26408C;--ltx-bg-color:#EDF2FC;">ls | grep</span> to collect the yearly editions of a table, the tool was revised to return all editions of a matched table together.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across all experiments, most artifacts are catalogs, which answer which file is relevant, or value indexes, which answer which file contains a given value. What exactly is built varies from one evolution run to another. For example, one value index inverts the cell values themselves and reaches 6 GB, whereas another is a full-text index over names, headers, and sampled cells. Other artifacts also occur, such as manifests that record which files belong together and in what order, and decoded copies of archives that were mis-stored as text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How tools help.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, tools save steps, which is why <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> is particularly effective under a tight step budget. At test time the Worker invoked <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">search_dataset_catalog</span> in all 80 held-out tasks. On the task requesting the 2018 violent crime rate of Albany County, every <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> repeat finished within four steps. One catalog query located the county-level crime-rate table, one SQL call through the companion <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">query_tabular_file</span> tool returned 359.8, and the Worker submitted the answer. Without such a tool, the baseline used nine to ten steps in every repeat, most of them on <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#26408C;--ltx-bg-color:#EDF2FC;">find</span> and <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#26408C;--ltx-bg-color:#EDF2FC;">grep</span> over the lake. The additional steps did not translate into accuracy, since in two of the three repeats the search selected an incorrect crime table and the baseline did not answer correctly. Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix D Synthetic-Question Scenario Across Workers ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows one repeat of each.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, tools make accuracy more robust, because a tool’s output is consistent across repeats while the Worker’s manual work is not. On the task asking for the calcium reading at the smallest dust-proxy value in a paleoclimate workbook, <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#0D7326;--ltx-bg-color:#EBF7EB;">inspect_excel_workbook</span> detected the header on the sixth row and returned the columns under their real names, so every <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> repeat filtered the two relevant columns with short scripts and answered correctly. The baseline, with nine steps in every repeat but without reusable tools, did not reach a correct answer. In one repeat it parsed the workbook manually and misaligned the header, and in another it located the file but failed to parse the header. Figure <a href="#A4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix D Synthetic-Question Scenario Across Workers ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows one repeat of each.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Tool Statistics</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix B Tool Statistics ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Tool Statistics ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> list the five most used tools in the libraries evolved for each lake with Gemini-3.1-Pro as the Worker. We run each test task three times and call each run an episode. The coverage of a tool is the percentage of test episodes in which the Worker called that tool at least once. On both lakes the most used tools answer where the data lives.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix B Tool Statistics ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, three of the KramaBench tools help the Worker find data. One maps a topic to the files that hold it, one searches for a column name or a value across all files, including Excel workbooks that <span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#26408C;--ltx-bg-color:#EDF2FC;">grep</span> cannot read, and one lists the files in the lake. The other two load files that standard readers parse incorrectly, namely CSVs that stack several tables in one file and Excel sheets whose header is not on the first row. The LakeQA lake is larger, with over four thousand files. As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Tool Statistics ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, its library therefore indexes the lake ahead of time. One tool looks up a pre-built catalog that records where each dataset is and which columns and format it has, and another searches a pre-built full-text index of dataset names and column headers. Its compute tool runs a filter or an aggregate over a large file through DuckDB, which streams the file instead of loading it into memory. The Worker calls the catalog tool in nearly every LakeQA episode, so almost every solution starts by querying the catalog rather than walking the directory tree.</p>
</div>
