<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Vertex AI list prices (USD per million tokens) used to estimate the cost of a test episode.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the cost of each test episode from three token counts. The <em id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uncached input</em> is the number of tokens written to the prompt cache for the first time, the <em id="A3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cached input</em> is the number of tokens read back from the cache on later turns, and the <em id="A3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output</em> is the number of completion tokens, including hidden reasoning, which both providers bill as output. We assume an optimal caching policy. The prefix, consisting of the system prompt, the tool schemas, and the skills, stays the same across all test episodes with the same method, Worker, lake, and step budget, so it is always read from the cache. Each turn then writes only the tokens added since the previous turn and reads the rest from the cache. The cost of an episode is then</p>
<table id="A3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=n_{\mathrm{u}}\,p_{\mathrm{u}}+n_{\mathrm{c}}\,p_{\mathrm{c}}+n_{\mathrm{o}}\,p_{\mathrm{o}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=n_{\mathrm{u}}\,p_{\mathrm{u}}+n_{\mathrm{c}}\,p_{\mathrm{c}}+n_{\mathrm{o}}\,p_{\mathrm{o}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the uncached input, cached input, and output token counts of the episode, and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding Vertex AI list prices of the Worker, given in Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.2 Cost accounting ‣ Appendix C Steps and Cost ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Since it assumes no cache misses, this estimate is a lower bound. We report this estimate instead of the billed cost because prompt caching behaved differently across Workers in our runs, so the billed cost mostly reflects the caching configuration of the client, whereas the estimate applies the same accounting to every Worker and method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27327v1/cost_accuracy.svg" id="A3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Accuracy versus estimated inference cost per test episode.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ C.2 Cost accounting ‣ Appendix C Steps and Cost ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is read in the same way as Figure <a href="#A3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ C.1 Step counting ‣ Appendix C Steps and Cost ‣ Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, except that the horizontal axis is the mean cost per episode, so a curve closer to the upper left again offers a better trade-off. With Sonnet-5, Gemini-3.1-Pro, and Gemini-3.8-Flash as the Worker, <span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> generally reaches higher accuracy at the same cost, or the same accuracy at a lower cost. Opus-4.8 has the most expensive input tokens, which limits the cost advantage of <span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>, but <span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> remains the better choice when the goal is higher accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Three factors shape the cost of <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>. First, <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> adds several tools and their skills to the prompt, so its fixed prefix is roughly two to three times as long as the baseline’s, and the Worker reads this prefix again at every step. Prompt caching absorbs much of this overhead, so we recommend enabling it when deploying <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span>, and we hope that future work on more effective caching will further reduce the cost of such repeated input. Second, perhaps surprisingly, when <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> shortens an episode, the saving appears in uncached input rather than in output. The result of a tool or shell command is billed as new input at the next step, and an evolved tool returns a compact result in place of the raw intermediate output that the baseline passes through the shell. On KramaBench at 15 steps, for example, <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TEAL</span> cuts the uncached input per episode by 37% to 63% for Sonnet-5, Gemini-3.1-Pro, and Gemini-3.8-Flash. Third, the output shrinks much less than the number of steps, since the output per step grows for most Workers. We attribute this to the Worker reasoning more at each step, plausibly about which of the additional tools to call.</p>
</div>
</section>
